{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md collab/1788962937302-architect-space-schema-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, fundamental primitives (tuples, streams), design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams. Persistent facts are immutable tuples and datoms `[e a v t m]`.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is events on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata).\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly. Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in Yin.VM that communicate with the outside world through DaoStream. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/agent.tzu.md`](./agent.tzu.md) and [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for agent and VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nHost-specific boundaries are isolated with DaoStream and interpreters of\nDaoStream. A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call.\n\nDaoStream handle operations are the one sanctioned function-shaped contact\nwith that boundary: their exhaustive outcome maps are stream protocol, not an\napplication callback. Above those operations, portable code still expresses\nhost work as effect values and observes outcomes as stream data.\n\nPortable code appends an effect value describing what must happen. A host\ninterpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and appends the\noutcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by identity in the\ndata; neither side holds a reference to the other.\n\nAn adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an\ninterpreter. It isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the\neffect never appears as an emission.\n\nA callback is an event on a stream. That is the same rule in the other\ndirection: when the host initiates -- a socket receives, a connection drops, a\ntimer fires -- the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**An adapter is a map of those functions. That is all an adapter is.** It is a\nvalue, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files, timers and windows\nall deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is what they call. The\nhost library is handed the map. Each entry takes the host's arguments and\nreturns one value; the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry\ncannot return a value to the host and cannot invoke application code. The\nadapter is handed only the deposit operation and its own host resource. If its\ndeposit channel fails, it may close that resource to make the boundary\nobservably gone; this is teardown, not an upward callback. Host types are\nclassified into plain data here -- a transform is the last place holding a host\nerror object, and no host type may cross onto the stream.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read, and it does\nnot need to. This is what keeps the layering one-way: a boundary depends on\nnothing above it, because whoever constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of those events semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol\nvalidation happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport\nmachinery, not an event the adapter is obliged to deposit. Once the transport\nhas produced a valid host event, data is syntax (axiom 2), so worth is not a\nproperty of the datum -- an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to\nanother that does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys\nthe perspectives that have not been taken. How much is retained is the medium's\nretention policy, which is operational; what an event means is interpretation.\nNeither is the depositor's decision.\n\nAn adapter that takes a function to invoke has relocated the callback, not\nremoved it, even when every value it passes is plain data. An adapter whose\ncallback has a meaningful return value is worse: that is a synchronous call in\nboth directions wearing the shape of an event.\n\nHost events are ordinary values on an ordinary stream. A transport may use a\nqualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and additive evolution;\nthat envelope remains plain data and creates no separate event mechanism. A\ncomposition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary for facts that it\nowns. Neither requires a bespoke bus or callback API.\n\nHost error types never cross the boundary. A transform function classifies them\nand emits a qualified value.\n\nA host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported\nresult. That is a correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\nFor advanced topics (parallel transport, capability tokens, entanglement):\nSee [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md)\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/website.md`](../agents/website.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-09 14:14 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-09 21:14 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 7762fd3e-1c6b-4e95-9e2d-2032a2209d5f\n\n# Plan: dao.space.schema → dao.stream\n\nRepository `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld`, branch `dao.stream-redesign-v2`,\nverified against `4b9f0e7`. Planning only; no file other than this one is\nwritten.\n\nMethod as before: the **invariants list** (§2) is the contract, grouped by\nthe test that exercises each and marked `[D]` stated in the design, `[T]`\npinned only by a test, `[T→D]` test-pinned and promoted to the design, `[T✗]`\nan accident of the v1 stream dressing dropped with its reason, and `[D✗]` a\nstated clause retired because the value model discharges it. The current\nimplementation and tests have no authority beyond the invariants they pin.\nNothing is in production. This plan is deleted when nothing in it is owed.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Corrections to the brief\n\nThe brief's measurements are right in every particular I could check, with\nfive refinements:\n\n1. **The 8-API / 16-line table is exact**, line for line. Two v1 touch points\n   sit outside it because they are not `ds/` calls: the `[dao.stream :as ds]`\n   require at `schema.cljc:19` and the `#?(:cljs (:require-macros\n   [dao.stream]))` at `:20`. Both go, and the four `#_{:clj-kondo/ignore …}`\n   forms at `:244`, `:305`, `:355`, `:1202` exist only to quiet kondo about\n   `ds/` vars behind reader conditionals; they go with the calls they cover.\n2. **`ds/strict-vec` in `schema_test` is prose, not code.** Its one\n   occurrence (`:615`) is inside a comment on `borrowed-path-does-not-close-\n   again`. Code-level v1 use in the test is: 63 `ds/close!`, 2 `ds/open!`\n   (`:305`, `:1630`), 1 `ds/append!` (`:306`), 1 `ds/closed?` (`:708`).\n3. **61 of the 63 `ds/close!` sites become `schema/close!`, not 63.** Two\n   target v1 fixtures rather than the wrapper — `:307` closes the v1 ring\n   buffer in the `open-closed` helper and `:621` hand-closes a\n   `RecordingStream` — and both die with the fixtures they close (§5).\n4. **Receipt assertion sites: 24 lines**, not the 20–23 earlier plans\n   counted: `:711 :712 :715 :780 :808 :843 :860 :940 :944 :976 :1006 :1062\n   :1071 :1119 :1120 :1179 :1333 :1348 :1383 :1796 :1881 :1940 :1957 :1959`.\n   Plus one `{:woke []}` pin at `:1134`. (`:1531`'s `{:result r}` is the\n   test observer's own ex-info data, not a receipt.)\n5. **The demo's route to `query` is direct, not via `semantic`.**\n   `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/compilation_pipeline.cljs:13` requires\n   `dao.space.query` itself (and `dao.space.transact`, and v1 `dao.stream` +\n   `dao.stream.ringbuffer` at `:15-16`). The conclusion the brief asked me to\n   confirm holds: **no file under `src/cljs`, `src/cljd` or `src/clj`\n   requires `dao.space.schema`**; the only in-tree references outside\n   `schema.cljc`, `schema_test.cljc` and `schema_fixtures.cljc` are a\n   docstring mention in `query.cljc:175` and prose in design docs. The demo\n   is unaffected by anything in this plan and is compiled each phase anyway\n   (§7).\n\nOne observation the brief did not make and the plan depends on: **schema\nalready accepts an opened published index on its value path.** `current`\ntests `(query/value? d)` (`:337`, `:345`), and `query/value?` answers true\nfor `{:dao.space.query/published …}`; `interpret-view` then calls\n`query/rows` on a `history` view over it, which forces the `:rows` delay.\nSo `(schema/current (query/open-published! coord))` works at `4b9f0e7`\nwithout a line changed — only the tests never exercise it, because W38–W41\nreach the manifest through `schema/published` and the `defopen` instead.\nThat is why Decision 5 can delete the opener rather than replace it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What schema is\n\n`dao.space.schema` is two clients of the tuple space sharing one extraction\nfunction. The **validating wrapper** (`schema/transactor`, `transact!`,\n`publish!`, `close!`) owns a `dao.space.transactor` value and, per\ntransaction, plans the complete next state — translation, lookup-ref\nresolution, supersession emission, both-modes structure checks, strict-mode\ndata checks — before exactly one inner append, installing the planned state\nonly when the inner receipt is `ok`. The **schema view** (`schema/current`)\nis a pure function from a d5 source value to a d3 fact-relation: extract the\nschema with `q` over the history view, current-state-resolve the data,\ncollapse the attributes the schema names card-one, pass everything else\nthrough. Neither client reads a live stream: the wrapper reads its local\nhandle through `index/snapshot-datoms` (already v2, T18), and the view takes\nvalues. Everything v1 about the namespace is dressing: a `deftype` wearing\n`IDaoStreamBound` so `ds/close!`/`ds/closed?` dispatch to it, a\n`defopen`-routed descriptor for the view, a borrowed-realization branch that\ndrains a v1 reader into a relation, and a `defopen` + reader record that\nturns a manifest into a v1 stream so `schema/current` can drain it back into\na vector. Taking the dressing off leaves a namespace that requires\n`dao.datom`, `dao.space.index`, `dao.space.query` and `dao.space.transactor`\n— and **neither `dao.stream` nor `dao.stream`**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The invariants\n\nNumbering continues the design's §9 matrix loosely; test names are the\ncurrent `schema_test` deftests. A `[T✗]`/`[D✗]` entry names its reason in\n§3.\n\n### 2.1 Representation and extraction — untouched by this plan\n\n`axioms-shape`, `bootstrap-expands-to-axioms`, `extraction-*`,\n`retracted-property-absent`, `reassert-after-retract`, `same-t-supersession-\nresolves-new-value`, `full-tie-collapses-to-least-v`, `unknown-db-type-\nthrows`, `unknown-db-star-name-ignored`, `meta-properties-resolve-from-\naxioms`, `conflicting-property-history-throws`, `no-ident-property-rows-\nignored`, `ident-tie-break-resolves-to-least-v`, `reified-metadata-reference-\nsurvives-schema-extraction` (15 deftests, `:46-:288`).\n\n| # | Invariant | |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | The five `:db/*` axioms, their fixed property maps, genesis ids 16–20, and `bootstrap`'s tx-data shape | `[D]` §2 |\n| E2 | Extraction runs through the public `q` surface over the history view; the private fold applies retraction, greatest `t`, least-`v` tie; `m` never orders | `[D]` §4 |\n| E3 | Unknown `:db.type` throws; unknown `:db/*` name is ignored by the view; meta-properties resolve from axioms | `[D]` §2, §3.1 |\n\n**No test in this group touches v1 and none changes.** They are listed so\nthe phase proofs can say \"unchanged\" and mean something.\n\n### 2.2 The read view — `schema/current`\n\n| # | Invariant | Test | |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| V1 | Card-one collapse: greatest `t`, least-`v` tie, never throws; out-of-order retraction resolves `v2`; same-`t` assert+retract resolves `v_new` | `view-collapses-card-one`, `out-of-order-…`, `same-t-assert-retract-…`, `reader-tie-never-throws` | `[D]` §4 |\n| V2 | Unschematized attributes pass through; undeclared cardinality is card-many; `:db/*` rows collapse by axiom with or without bootstrap rows | `unschematized-…`, `undeclared-cardinality-…`, `schema-rows-themselves-collapse` | `[D]` §3, §4 |\n| V3 | `:as-of` bounds data and schema; `:schema-as-of` re-reads the schema at another point of the same `t` axis | `as-of-bounds-data-and-schema`, `schema-as-of-rereads-schema` | `[D]` §4 |\n| V4 | The result is a query fact-relation value (`query/value?`, `:fact? true`) that `q` accepts directly | `schema-current-returns-a-fact-relation-value` | `[T→D]` — §4 still says \"returns a self-contained closed `ViewStream`\"; promote the value statement |\n| V5 | A nested `query/current` or `query/history` view is rejected as a source | `nested-view-descriptor-rejected` (cases 1–2) | `[D]` §4, §8 |\n| V6 | A schema view's own output is rejected as a source | `nested-view-descriptor-rejected` (case 3) | `[T]`, **kept, restated on the value**: case 3 today builds a v1 `:dao.space.schema/current` descriptor by hand; it becomes \"a `:fact?` relation is rejected\" (§3, D3) |\n| V7 | Unknown `:db.type` surfaces when the view is built | `unknown-db-type-throws-at-realization` | `[D]` §3.1; only the comment (\"when q opens the view descriptor\") is stale |\n| V8 | The descriptor path and the borrowed-realization path answer identically | `borrowed-and-descriptor-paths-agree` | **`[T✗]`** — D3/D6 |\n| V9 | A borrowed source must satisfy `IDaoStreamBound` and answer `closed?` true | `current` `:320-:327` (untested negative) | **`[T✗]`** — D6; query's I6 ruling |\n| V10 | The `defopen` route opens `:source`, closes it exactly once, interprets | `inner-stream-closed-after-descriptor-path` | **`[T✗]`** — D3: there is no opening, so there is no inner stream to close |\n| V11 | The borrowed path never closes its source | `borrowed-path-does-not-close-again` | **`[T✗]`** — D6: the value model has no `close!` to abstain from |\n| V12 | `:dao.stream/bound` inherited from `:source` | (already gone; W11's comment records it) | `[D✗]` — retired with query's descriptors; §4's descriptor block is deleted (§6) |\n| V13 | **New.** A v2 reader becomes a schema source only through `query/snapshot`; `schema/current` accepts the snapshot result when its status is `:ended` or `:blocked` and rejects `:gap`/`:defect` | new: `snapshot-is-the-only-live-source` | **`[D]`, new** — D4 |\n| V14 | **New.** `schema/current` over an opened published index forces the rows before returning; the result stays valid after `close-published!` | new: `schema-view-is-self-contained-after-close-published` | **`[T→D]`, new** — the value-model form of §9's \"no inner-stream leak after `close!` (the ownership trap)\" |\n\n### 2.3 The write wrapper — lifecycle and results\n\n| # | Invariant | Test | |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1 | `schema/transactor` creates and owns the inner transactor value; it never leaks | `wrapper-opens-and-transacts` and the closed test | `[D]` §3.1 |\n| L2 | Closing the wrapper closes the inner value and neither closes nor erases the caller's local stream | `closed-wrapper-throws` (second assertion) | `[D]` §3.1 + transactor T7 |\n| L3 | `close!` returns `{:woke []}` | `publish-serializes-against-close` `:1134` | **`[T✗]`** — D1 |\n| L4 | `closed?` is a public predicate | `wrapper-opens-and-transacts` `:708` | **`[T✗]`** — D2; v2 *Explicitly Absent* |\n| L5 | `transact!` on a closed wrapper throws | `closed-wrapper-throws` | **`[T✗]`** → replaced by L5′: answers `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` as data — D1 |\n| L6 | The ok receipt is `{:result :ok :t t :datoms ds}` | the 24 sites of §0.4 | **`[T✗]`** → replaced by L6′: the inner v2 receipt `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}` passes through unchanged — D1 |\n| L7 | A conforming non-ok inner outcome is returned unchanged; the wrapper's state does not advance; the same `t` is retried | `failed-inner-append-leaves-wrapper-state-unchanged` | `[D]` §3.1 (transactor T19) — **kept verbatim**; only the success literal at `:1071` changes shape |\n| L8 | `publish!` delegates to the inner `publish!` and returns `{:manifest-address … :manifest …}` | W38–W41 | `[D]` §5, transactor T11 |\n| L9 | `publish!` on a closed wrapper throws; the guard is serialized with the publication under the wrapper lock | `publish-serializes-against-close` | **`[T✗]`** — D7 |\n| L10 | Argument defects throw with nothing appended: every rejection listed in §3 and §3.1 | every `thrown-with-msg?` in the wrapper section (`:769-:1966`) | `[D]` §3 — **unchanged**; this is the boundary D1 does not cross |\n\n### 2.4 The write wrapper — semantics, untouched by this plan\n\n`map-form-supersession`, `add-collision-…`, `valueType-…`, `ref-existence`,\n`lookup-ref-in-db-id`, `lookup-ref-in-ref-value`, `no-dedup`, `card-many-\nexpansion`, `retract-attribute-wide`, `schema-evolves-through-wrapper`,\n`bootstrap-reads-back`, `bootstrap-verbatim-retransact-allowed`, `unknown-\ndb-star-name-rejected-both-modes`, `illegal-schema-values-…`, `duplicate-\nident-…`, `axiom-protection`, `user-schema-evolution-allowed`, `unique-\nrequires-card-one-…`, `lookup-ref-after-same-record-…`, `re-extract-gated`,\n`audit-via-fns`, `schema-in-source`, `unique-duplicate-…`, `lax-unique-\nambiguity-…`, `retract-frees-unique-value`, `map-form-repairs-…`,\n`concurrent-strict-unique-writes-serialize`, `non-emitting-map-…`,\n`opposite-operations-…`, `schema-retractions-update-…`, `reified-metadata-\nreference-seeds-wrapper-state`, `unique-is-per-stream-not-per-world`.\n\n| # | Invariant | |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | Every §3 / §3.1 / §3.2 / §3.3 ruling: mode split, supersession shape, lookup-ref resolution, axiom protection, no-dedup, atomicity, serialization, epoch-on-next-transaction, reopen parity, the per-stream guarantee boundary | `[D]` |\n\nTheir **only** edits are mechanical: `(ds/close! w)` → `(schema/close! w)`\nand `(:result r)`/`(:t r)`/`(:datoms r)` → the v2 keys. No assertion's\nmeaning changes.\n\n### 2.5 The publisher — `publish!`, the published coordinate, parity\n\n| # | Invariant | Test | |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `q` answers identically before and after publish, through the schema view | `publish-then-schema-view-parity` | `[D]` §9 |\n| P2 | The published source answers the same raw query as the drained relation | `published-descriptor-as-raw-db-input` | `[D]` §9 |\n| P3 | The published source is accepted as `schema/current`'s `:source` | `published-accepted-as-current-source` | `[D]` §7 — kept, on `query/open-published!`'s value |\n| P4 | `schema/published` validates its coordinate arguments; the opener rejects a coordinate with extra keys | `published-descriptor-validation` | **`[T✗]`** — D5: the constructor and opener are deleted; `index/published-index` performs the same three argument checks (`index.cljc:332-337`) and `open-published-rejects-unresolvable-and-malformed-coordinates` (`query_test:936`) pins extra-key rejection on the surviving opener |\n| P5 | A `:dao.space.schema/published` coordinate is its own `:dao.stream/type`, realized by a registered opener over `PublishedSchemaRows` | design §5 | **`[D✗]`** — D5 |\n| P6 | A content store opened by the published opener is closed before the value is returned, on success and on failure (the uncovered `finally`, brief Q8) | none | **`[D✗]`** — D5/D8: the code that owned the store is deleted; ownership moves to the caller, where `failed-open-closes-the-store-it-opened` (`query_test:1076`) and `close-published-closes-once-and-is-idempotent` (`:716`) already pin it |\n\n### 2.6 Fixtures\n\n| # | Invariant | |\n|---|---|---|\n| F1 | A `defopen` registration cannot live in a ClojureDart test namespace, so it lives in `schema_fixtures` | **`[T✗]`** — D9: there is no registration |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Decisions\n\n### D1 — The wrapper is a value with named operations; results are the inner transactor's, unwrapped. (Brief Q1, Q2)\n\n`SchemaWrapper` implements one v1 protocol to expose two operations, and\nboth exist to make `ds/close!` and `ds/closed?` dispatch. In the value model\nthe transactor already made (`dao.space.transactor.md`, *A transactor is a\nvalue, not a stream*), the wrapper becomes the same kind of thing: a plain\nmap, not a `deftype`:\n\n```clojure\n(schema/transactor local intake {:strict true})\n;; => {:dao.space.schema/transactor true\n;;     :inner        <transactor value>      ; owned\n;;     :local-stream local                   ; borrowed, for proposed-schema's snapshot\n;;     :strict?      true\n;;     :state        (atom {…, :closed false})}\n\n(schema/transact! w tx-data)\n;; ok      => {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms ds}\n;; refused => the inner outcome map, unchanged (full | closed | invalid-value | transport-error)\n;; closed  => {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}\n;; defect  => throws, nothing appended (unchanged)\n(schema/publish! w opts)   ; => {:manifest-address … :manifest …}   (unchanged)\n(schema/close! w)          ; => {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}, idempotent\n```\n\n**The D10 collapse is a deletion, not a translation.** `transact!`'s\nsuccess branch currently rebuilds `{:result :ok :t … :datoms …}` from the\ninner receipt; it now returns the inner receipt. The two-shaped outcome\nspace (`:result` versus `:dao.stream/outcome`) that the D10 rule created as\na named transitional artifact collapses to one shape, and the 24 assertion\nlines in §0.4 are edited by rule: `(:result r)` → `(:dao.stream/outcome r)`\nagainst `:dao.stream/ok`; `(:t r)` → `(:dao.space/t r)`; `(:datoms r)` →\n`(:dao.space/datoms r)`; the literal at `:1071` becomes the v2 literal.\n\n**Why schema adds nothing to the receipt.** It could — the wrapper knows\nthings the transactor does not (the epoch it installed, the repair datoms it\nemitted) — but `:dao.space/datoms` already carries the emitted datoms\nincluding repairs (`map-form-repairs-all-card-one-values` reads them), and an\nepoch marker is a feature with no consumer. Open maps permit adding keys\nlater without breaking anyone; nothing is reserved.\n\n**`close!` returns the v2 close outcome.** `{:woke []}` was v1's waiter\nvocabulary and v2 has no waiters (*Explicitly Absent: waiter registration*).\nIt is idempotent because the inner `tx/close!` is and the flag is a `swap!`\nto `true`.\n\n**Closed → data, defects → throw. The line is the transactor's line.**\n`transact!` on a closed wrapper currently throws; it now answers\n`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}` first, before any argument is\nlooked at, exactly as `tx/transact!` does. Everything else that throws in\n`transact!` — empty tx-data, zero datoms, every §3 rejection — **still\nthrows**, because those are defects in the caller's argument detected before\nany stream is touched. This is not a softening of schema's central claim\n(\"a rejected transaction throws with nothing appended\", §3); closedness is\nnot a rejection of the transaction, it is a fact about the wrapper. The\norder matters and is pinned: closed check, then argument validation, so a\nclosed wrapper never throws an argument diagnostic for a transaction it would\nnot have appended anyway.\n\n### D2 — `closed?` does not survive. (Brief Q2)\n\nThe brief asks whether the wrapper's flag being \"real per-wrapper state\nrather than a stale predicate over a remote handle\" earns `closed?` a stay.\nIt does not, for two reasons that hold even though the flag is local:\n\n- **The staleness argument is about the caller, not the flag.** A caller\n  that reads `closed?` and then calls `transact!` has two operations with a\n  window between them; on the JVM the wrapper's own lock does not cover the\n  caller's `if`. The contract's replacement — \"operation results are\n  authoritative\" — is what closes that window, and `transact!` now supplies\n  the answer as data (L5′).\n- **The flag stays; the predicate goes.** The wrapper keeps `:closed` in its\n  state atom because `transact!` needs it to answer `closed` (and `publish!`\n  no longer needs it, D7). What is deleted is the public read of it. The\n  `wrapper-state` test helper still reads the atom directly for T19's\n  state-identity assertion; that is a white-box test seam, not a predicate.\n\n`wrapper-opens-and-transacts:708` loses its `(is (not (ds/closed? w)))`;\nthe successful `transact!` two lines later already proves the wrapper is\nopen. `closed-wrapper-throws` becomes `closed-wrapper-answers-closed`, and\nits second assertion — the local stream still `blocked`s at its tail, so the\ncaller's handle was not closed — stays verbatim (L2).\n\n### D3 — The `:dao.space.schema/current` descriptor route dies; callers pass values. (Brief Q3)\n\n`current` is dual today: a query value interprets immediately; a map with a\nkeyword `:dao.stream/type` is wrapped in a `:dao.space.schema/current`\ndescriptor and pushed through `ds/open!`, whose `defopen` body opens\n`:source`, closes it, and interprets. The registry is *Explicitly Absent*,\nand query's Decision 1 (inputs are values) is the precedent. Follow it.\n\n**What is lost, honestly.** Three things the route could do that the value\npath cannot:\n\n1. **Accept a source it did not know how to read.** Any `open!`-dispatchable\n   descriptor — a raw v1 ring buffer, `schema.test/recording`, the old\n   index adapter — went through. In v2 the transports schema might meet are\n   reached by `attach!` on a handle the *caller* holds, and reading them is\n   a `query/snapshot` (D4). Nothing schema-side needs to know a transport.\n2. **Own the inner stream's lifetime.** The `defopen` body closed what it\n   opened. In the value model there is nothing to open; an opened published\n   index is closed by whoever opened it (D5). This is the same ownership\n   move query made and the same one `dao.space.transactor` made for its\n   local stream.\n3. **Be named across a serialization boundary.** A `:dao.space.schema/\n   current` descriptor was, on paper, data that could travel and be opened\n   elsewhere. It could not: its `:source` was a v1 descriptor carrying\n   either live state or its own data (the eliminated relation transport),\n   and its `:as-of` keys were interpretation, not reachability. A view is\n   an interpretation a reader applies, not a place a reader attaches to —\n   Axiom 2 says so directly. What *does* travel is the coordinate under it\n   (`index/published-index`, P2 in query's plan) plus the fact that the\n   receiver chose the schema lens.\n\nSo `current`'s contract becomes: `(current source)` / `(current source\n{:as-of n :schema-as-of n})` where `source` is a relation value, an opened\npublished index, or a `query/snapshot` result (D4). A `query/current` /\n`query/history` view is rejected (V5, unchanged). **A `:fact?` relation is\nrejected** (V6, restated): `nested-view-descriptor-rejected`'s third case\npasses `(schema/current rel)`'s own result back in, which replaces the\nhand-built v1 descriptor the case constructs today. Any other value falls\nthrough to `query/history`'s own rejection (\"query db input must be a\nrelation value, a datom view, or an opened published index\"), which is the\ninformative error query's I1 already guarantees; schema does not duplicate\nit. `validate-not-nested-view!` loses its `(= t :dao.space.schema/current)`\narm and gains the `:fact?` arm.\n\n### D4 — The v1 drain vanishes; a live stream enters only through `query/snapshot`, and schema states its own completeness policy. (Brief Q4)\n\n`interpret-view:260-261` converts a v1 realization to a relation because\nquery takes values. With D3 and D6 no realization can reach it, so the\nbranch is deleted and `interpret-view` takes a query value, full stop.\n\n**Is a live stream a legitimate schema source?** Yes — the same way it is\nfor query: a caller with a v2 reader handle calls `query/snapshot` and hands\nschema the result. `schema/current` **does not** take a handle and does not\ncall `snapshot` itself; schema opens nothing and reads nothing (query's\nDecision 1 and 3, kept whole). The drain does not \"become `query/snapshot`\nfor a genuine v2 handle\" inside schema; it moves to the caller.\n\n**Where schema departs from query, with the reason.** `query/current`\naccepts any snapshot result and lets the caller decide whether a `:gap`\nsuffix is a relation it can query (\"query has no basis to decide that for\nit\"). Schema has a basis. The schema rows live at the origin of the history\n— the bootstrap is \"the stream's genesis transaction\" (§2) — so a snapshot\nthat lost its prefix has, with high probability, lost the vocabulary; the\nview would then silently degrade to raw pass-through with card-one collapse\ndisabled, which is a wrong answer that looks like a right one. And schema's\nown design already says \"schema interprets the whole history at once\" (§5).\nSo `schema/current` accepts a snapshot result with status `:ended` or\n`:blocked` (a closed stream fully read; an open stream caught up) and\n**throws** on `:gap` and `:defect`, naming the status. This is the policy\n`dao.space.query.md` says each caller keeps for itself (\"query treats a\n`gap` as a usable partial relation, and `dao.space.index` aborts publication\non one. Each caller keeps its own\"); schema's is index's, for index's\nreason. V13 pins it with a ring buffer fixture in `schema_test`: fill past\ncapacity, snapshot, assert the throw; append to an open memory-log,\nsnapshot, assert `:blocked` is accepted and the view answers.\n\n### D5 — `:dao.space.schema/published`, `schema/published`, `PublishedSchemaRows` and the opener are deleted; nothing replaces them. (Brief Q5, Q8)\n\nThe brief asks what replaces the `defopen` and where the opener belongs.\nThe answer is that **the opener already exists and is query's.** Per §0's\nobservation, `(schema/current (query/open-published! (index/published-index\nstore manifest-address)))` works today. What the schema-typed coordinate\nbought was a `:dao.stream/type` for `ds/open!` to dispatch on. Remove the\ndispatch and the type has no job.\n\nThree further reasons not to keep a schema-typed coordinate, in decreasing\nweight:\n\n1. **It contradicts §1.** \"A raw dao.space agent and a schema'd agent share\n   one medium and read the same log differently … Schema is a lens, not a\n   cage.\" A coordinate stamped `:dao.space.schema/published` bakes the\n   reader's lens into the name of the data. The lens is applied by the\n   reader, at read time, by calling `schema/current`; the coordinate is the\n   same `index/published-index` value for both readers. §5 already half-says\n   this: \"The manifest is schema-independent.\"\n2. **It is not reachability data.** `schema/published` produced the same\n   five keys as `index/published-index` with a different type keyword — a\n   value with a transport type stamped on it, the exact shape query's\n   Decision 2 retired (\"a descriptor that *is* the data is not reachability\n   data\").\n3. **A second opener is a second store-ownership model.** `PublishedSchemaRows`\n   existed because schema wanted eager rows and a store closed before\n   return; query's `open-published!` defers the rows and hands the store to\n   the caller. Two openers over one manifest format, differing in ownership,\n   is the \"undecided coexistence\" the v2 doc calls a defect. Query's model\n   wins because it is the one with consumers (`stigmergy_test`'s `sources`,\n   every `query_test` published test) and because eager-versus-lazy is a\n   caller's choice already available: `schema/current` forces the rows (it\n   must, to extract the schema), so the caller closes the store right after\n   it returns (V14).\n\n**No `schema/open-published!` convenience either.** It would be a one-liner\nthat hides a `close-published!` the caller must still make, and it would\nput an opener in schema, which is exactly the question the brief asks and\nthis decision answers \"nowhere new.\"\n\n**The uncovered `finally` (Q8) is discharged by deletion.** The debt the\ntransactor Phase 3 record named — \"removing schema's separate `finally`\nwould require a schema-specific test\" — was owed on code this plan removes.\nThere is no schema-side resource ownership left to test: `schema/current`\nover an opened index acquires nothing. The property itself (a store opened\nduring a failed open is closed before the error propagates; a successful\nopen's store is closed exactly once by its owner) is pinned on the surviving\nopener by `failed-open-closes-the-store-it-opened` (`query_test:1076`) and\n`close-published-closes-once-and-is-idempotent` (`:716`). Stated here so the\ntransactor record's note can be closed by pointer rather than left dangling.\n\nConsequently `schema.cljc` drops its `dao.jing` and `dao.jing.coordinate`\nrequires: `jing/segment-address?` was used only by `schema/published`, and\n`jing-coordinate/open!` only by the opener.\n\n### D6 — The borrowed-realization contract is an accident of the dressing. Same ruling as query's I6. (Brief Q6)\n\n`current:319-327` demands a v1 realization satisfy `IDaoStreamBound` and\nanswer `closed?` true, then drains it. Query dropped its equivalent because\n\"a row vector computed in full has none of those needs — no second\nobserver, no future appends, no eviction, no reachability.\" Schema's is not\ndifferent: the borrowed input was always a *finished* reader — the test\nhelper builds one by appending and closing a ring buffer, and\n`PublishedSchemaRows` was \"closed from construction.\" A finished reader is a\nvector wearing a protocol; `query/relation` is the honest spelling of it.\n\nThe one thing the borrowed path pinned that the value path did not was\n\"schema/current never closes a borrowed source\" (`borrowed-path-does-not-\nclose-again`). In the value model this is vacuous — there is no `close!`\nschema could call on a relation — and for the one source that *is* closable\n(an opened published index) V14 pins the real property: schema forces the\nrows and leaves closing to the owner. Both borrowed-path deftests and the\n`open-closed` helper go.\n\n### D7 — `publish!` loses its closed guard and its lock. (Brief Q1's third result, and the transactor plan's deferred question)\n\nThe transactor plan's D8 moved schema's `publish!` guard under the wrapper\nlock and deferred to this plan the alternative of \"redefining\npublication-after-close as permitted.\" Rule for the alternative:\n\n- **The inner value permits it.** `tx/publish!` (`transactor.cljc:271`) has\n  no closed check; T7 says close \"rejects further writes\" and says nothing\n  about publication, because publication is a *read* of the local stream\n  plus an enqueue into the pool — neither of which the wrapper's close\n  affects (L2: the local stream is the caller's and stays open).\n- **The guard is what created the race.** D8's lock extension existed so a\n  concurrent `close!` could not slip between the guard and the publication.\n  Delete the guard and the race has nothing to protect; `publish!` becomes\n  the thin delegation its docstring already calls it, and the lock is no\n  longer held across an index build — the cost D8 called \"real.\"\n- **Datomic's shape.** Releasing a connection does not invalidate a `db`\n  value you already hold, and building an index over a log you can still\n  read is a read-side derivation.\n\n`publish-serializes-against-close` is `[T✗]`: every one of its four\nassertions is about the guard (publish blocked in flight, close waits,\n`{:woke []}`, publish-after-close refused). The one property in it worth\nkeeping — an in-flight publication completes with the full history — is a\nproperty of `index/publish-index!` over a complete-retention transport, not\nof the wrapper, and is pinned on the transactor's side by\n`publish-enqueues-indexes-into-the-pool`. The design's §3.1 sentence \"The\nclosedness guard is serialized with tx/publish! under the wrapper lock\"\nlives only in `publish!`'s docstring, not in the design doc; the design\ngains one sentence: publication after close is permitted and reads the\ncaller's still-open local stream.\n\n### D8 — The wrapper's white-box seam. (Consequence of D1, host matrix)\n\n`wrapper-state` (`schema_test:676-682`) reaches `(.-state w)` with a\nper-host type hint — the ClojureDart trap the memory records (a private\nmutable field on a `deftype`). With the wrapper a map, it becomes `@(:state\nw)` with no reader conditional. `transact!`'s `^SchemaWrapper` hints and\n`(.-inner wrapper)` / `(.-local-stream wrapper)` / `(.-strict? wrapper)`\nbecome destructuring. The `with-write-lock` helper is unchanged — it locks\nthe state atom, which is still an atom.\n\n### D9 — `schema_fixtures` is deleted, and its ClojureDart constraint no longer binds. (Brief Q7)\n\nThe namespace's docstring states its reason precisely: a `defopen`\nregistration is a *multimethod contribution* into `dao.stream/open!`, and on\nClojureDart a contribution emitted from a test namespace points at a path\nthe renamed test library does not occupy. The constraint is about\nregistration into an ambient table, not about `deftype` in test namespaces\nas such — `schema_test` already `reify`s v2 protocols inline at `:1041` and\n`:1093`, on all three hosts. With no `defopen` anywhere in schema (D3, D5)\nthere is nothing to register and nothing to relocate. Both consumers of the\nfixture (`inner-stream-closed-after-descriptor-path`, `borrowed-path-does-\nnot-close-again`) are `[T✗]` under D3/D6. The file goes.\n\n### D10 — Two phases, by seam; write side first. (Brief Q9)\n\nSchema has no in-tree consumer to keep compiling, so nothing *forces* a\nsplit, and the brief notes query moved in one piece. Split anyway, for a\nreason that is about review rather than compilation: the write side is ~85\nmechanical edits in the test file (61 `close!` sites, 24 receipt lines, a\n`{:woke []}`) over a `deftype`-to-map rewrite whose semantics (§2.4) must\nnot move, while the read side deletes two routes, a namespace and a\ncoordinate type and rewrites nine deftests. One commit mixing \"nothing\nsemantic changed\" with \"these seven tests are gone\" is a commit a reviewer\ncannot check by rule; two commits can each be checked by their residue grep.\n\nWrite side first, because after it the test file's remaining `ds/` count is\nexactly the read-side inventory (§5.3) and Phase 2's closure grep is then\nthe end condition. Either order is green; this one makes the second phase\nsmaller and its closure criterion unambiguous. W38–W41 are touched in both\nphases (their `ds/close! w` in Phase 1, their `schema/published` in Phase\n2) — four deftests edited twice, which is the whole cost of the split.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Phase 1 — the write side\n\n### Build\n\n`src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc`:\n\n- `transactor` returns the D1 map. Docstring: owns the inner value; the\n  wrapper's flag is its own; no `closed?`.\n- `transact!`: destructure the map; first branch under the lock is\n  `(if (:closed @state) {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed} …)`; the\n  success branch returns `result` (the inner receipt) unchanged; the non-ok\n  branch is unchanged. Docstring rewritten on the v2 receipt.\n- `close!`: new public fn — under the lock, `swap!` `:closed true`, `tx/close!`\n  on `:inner`, return `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}`.\n- `publish!`: `(tx/publish! (:inner w) opts)`. No guard, no lock (D7).\n- Delete `SchemaWrapper` and its `ds/IDaoStreamBound` implementation. The\n  `[dao.stream :as ds]` require **stays this phase** — `current` and the\n  openers still use it — as does the `:require-macros`.\n\n`docs/design/dao.space.schema.md`, in-phase:\n\n- §3.1's ownership paragraph (`:383-:392`): \"its `close!` closes the inner\n  value and returns the wrapper's own v1 shape `{:woke []}`\" → returns the\n  v2 close outcome; the `closed?` sentence becomes: there is no `closed?`;\n  `transact!` on a closed wrapper answers `closed` as data, the vocabulary\n  `dao.space.transactor` uses.\n- §3.1's D10 paragraph (`:394-:403`) is **deleted** and replaced by one\n  sentence: `transact!` returns the inner transactor's receipt unchanged —\n  ok, refused, or closed — and throws only for defects in the caller's\n  argument (§3's rejections).\n- §3.1 gains D7's sentence on publication after close.\n- §7's usage block: `(schema/transact! …)` comments show the v2 receipt;\n  add `(schema/close! log)`.\n- `docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md`: T20 becomes \"retired 2026-09-09\n  under schema's plan; schema returns the transactor's receipt unchanged\"\n  (keep the row, as T12/T13/T14 were kept in the plan's table, so the\n  numbering stays stable); the *Open items* bullet \"Schema's migration …\n  collapses the transitional mixed return\" is deleted.\n\n### Delete\n\n`SchemaWrapper`; the `{:result :ok …}` re-wrap; `publish!`'s guard and\nlock; `transact!`'s closed throw.\n\n### Tests\n\n`test/dao/space/schema_test.cljc`:\n\n- 61 `(ds/close! …)` on wrappers → `(schema/close! …)`. The two fixture\n  closes at `:307`, `:621` are Phase 2's.\n- 24 receipt lines per D1's rule.\n- `wrapper-state` per D8; `:708` deleted.\n- `closed-wrapper-throws` → `closed-wrapper-answers-closed`: assert the\n  `closed` outcome map; keep the local-stream `blocked` assertion.\n- `publish-serializes-against-close` deleted (D7, L9). Its `slow-local`\n  reify goes with it.\n- **New:** `close-is-idempotent-and-closes-the-inner-value` — two `close!`s\n  both answer `ok`; afterwards `transact!` answers `closed`; the local\n  stream still answers `blocked` at `newest` (L1, L2, D1).\n- **New:** `closed-check-precedes-argument-validation` — on a closed wrapper,\n  `(schema/transact! w [])` and `(schema/transact! w [[:db/add 30 :db/foo\n  :bar]])` both answer `closed` rather than throwing (D1's order pin).\n- **New:** `publish-after-close-reads-the-callers-stream` — transact,\n  close, publish; the manifest's `:count` equals the local history (D7).\n\n### Prove\n\n- Every §2.4 deftest passes with only the two mechanical edit classes\n  applied; a diff of the test file filtered to lines containing `close!`,\n  `:result`, `:t `, `:datoms`, `:dao.stream/`, `:dao.space/` accounts for\n  every changed line outside the deftests named above. That filter is the\n  reviewer's check that §2.4 moved nothing.\n- `failed-inner-append-leaves-wrapper-state-unchanged` passes with only its\n  `:1071` literal changed — T19 is the invariant most at risk from the\n  `deftype`→map rewrite, and this test is its pin.\n- `grep -c \"ds/\" src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc`: 16 → **11** (the three\n  wrapper sites `:946`, `:1079` and the protocol form go; `:946` counted\n  once). `grep -c \"ds/\" test/dao/space/schema_test.cljc`: 68 → **6**\n  (`:17` require, `:22` require-macros, `:305-:307`, `:615` prose, `:621`,\n  `:1630` — the read-side inventory).\n- `clojure -M:test`, `bb test:cljs` (confirm `Testing dao.space.schema-test`\n  in the node output), `bb test:cljd`, and `clj -M:cljs -m\n  shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile demo`.\n\n## 5. Phase 2 — the read side\n\n### Build\n\n`src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc`:\n\n- `interpret-view` takes a query value; the `ds/realization?` branch is\n  gone. Body otherwise unchanged.\n- `current`: the D3 contract. Shape:\n\n```clojure\n(defn current\n  ([source] (current source nil))\n  ([source opts]\n   (let [as-of (when (map? opts) (:as-of opts))\n         schema-as-of (when (map? opts) (:schema-as-of opts))\n         source (if (snapshot-result? source)      ; {:relation … :status …}\n                  (do (when-not (#{:ended :blocked} (:status source))\n                        (throw (ex-info \"schema/current requires a complete snapshot\"\n                                        {:status (:status source)})))\n                      (:relation source))\n                  source)]\n     (validate-not-nested-view! source)             ; query views, :fact? relations\n     (interpret-view source as-of schema-as-of))))  ; query/history rejects the rest\n```\n\n  `snapshot-result?` mirrors query's private `snapshot-value?` (a map with\n  `:relation` and `:status`); query's stays private and schema spells its\n  own two-line predicate rather than widening query's surface for one\n  caller.\n- `validate-not-nested-view!`: drop the `:dao.stream/type` arm, add the\n  `:fact?` arm, error text \"schema/current source must be a d5 source\n  value, not a nested view\".\n- Delete the `[dao.stream :as ds]`, `[dao.jing :as jing]` and\n  `[dao.jing.coordinate :as jing-coordinate]` requires and the\n  `#?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream]))`. Final require set:\n  `dao.datom`, `dao.space.index`, `dao.space.query`, `dao.space.transactor`.\n- Delete the four kondo-ignore forms.\n\n`docs/design/dao.space.schema.md`, in-phase:\n\n- §4's descriptor block (`:494-:507`) and the *Realization* paragraph\n  (`:509-:531`): replaced by the value contract — `schema/current` is a\n  function from a d5 source value (a relation value, an opened published\n  index, or a complete `query/snapshot` result) to a fact-relation value\n  `q` accepts; it opens and closes nothing; nested views and its own output\n  are rejected; D4's completeness policy with its reason.\n- §5's published-descriptor paragraph and block (`:568-:584`): deleted.\n  Replaced by: the published coordinate is `index/published-index`, opened\n  by `query/open-published!` and closed by its caller; `schema/current`\n  forces the rows before returning, so the caller may close immediately\n  (V14); there is no schema-typed coordinate because the lens is the\n  reader's (D5 reason 1).\n- §7: \"Both openers register at namespace load … fails closed otherwise\"\n  (`:625-:627`) deleted; the usage block's read example becomes\n  `open-published!` → `schema/current` → `close-published!`; the \"requires\n  `dao.space.schema` to be `require`d first\" sentence goes.\n- §8 *Implementation verification*: the `defopen`/`ViewStream`/`::owned`\n  sentence and \"`:source` is an `open!`-dispatchable d5 descriptor\" are\n  replaced by \"the view is a value; V13/V14\".\n- §9: \"the borrowed-realization path answers identically to the descriptor\n  path; no inner-stream leak after `close!`\" → V13 and V14's statements;\n  \"publisher parity: the published descriptor opens through its registered\n  opener\" → \"through `query/open-published!`\".\n- The status paragraph gets one sentence recording this migration and the\n  date.\n- `docs/design/dao.stream.md`'s consumer list (`:803-:805`): `dao.space` is\n  removed from the list entirely.\n- `docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md`: the *Related documents* line for\n  schema loses \"its D10 rule governs the shapes it re-wraps\".\n- `query.cljc:175`'s docstring \"dao.space.schema builds these directly\" is\n  still true; unchanged.\n\n### Delete\n\n`ds/defopen :dao.space.schema/current` and its body; the `ds/open!` call in\n`current`; `published`; `PublishedSchemaRows`; `ds/defopen\n:dao.space.schema/published`; **`test/dao/space/schema_fixtures.cljc`**\n(whole file, D9).\n\n### Tests\n\n`test/dao/space/schema_test.cljc`:\n\n- Requires: drop `dao.stream`, `dao.space.schema-fixtures`, the\n  `:require-macros`. `dao.stream`, `memory-log`, `ringbuffer` stay\n  (fixtures for the wrapper and for V13).\n- `open-closed` helper deleted.\n- `borrowed-and-descriptor-paths-agree`, `inner-stream-closed-after-\n  descriptor-path`, `borrowed-path-does-not-close-again` **deleted** (V8,\n  V10, V11).\n- `nested-view-descriptor-rejected` case 3: `(schema/current (schema/current\n  rel))` throws with the new message (V6).\n- `unknown-db-type-throws-at-realization`: comment rewritten; assertion\n  unchanged (V7).\n- `published-descriptor-validation` **deleted** (P4); its four properties\n  are named in §2.5 with their surviving pins.\n- W38, W39, W41: `(schema/published store-coord manifest-address)` →\n  `(query/open-published! (index/published-index store-coord\n  manifest-address))`, bound in the `let`, with `(query/close-published!\n  opened)` added to the existing `finally` beside `cleanup-file`. Assertions\n  unchanged (P1–P3). `ds/open!` at `:1630` goes with W40.\n- **New:** `snapshot-is-the-only-live-source` (V13): (a) a v2 ring buffer of\n  capacity 4 receives `schema-rows` plus data past capacity; `query/snapshot`\n  answers `:gap`; `schema/current` on it throws naming `:gap`. (b) An open\n  memory-log receives the same rows; `snapshot` answers `:blocked`;\n  `schema/current` answers the same as over `(query/relation rows)`. (c) The\n  same after `stream/close!` on the log: `:ended`, same answer.\n- **New:** `schema-view-is-self-contained-after-close-published` (V14):\n  publish, materialize, `open-published!`, `schema/current`, **then**\n  `close-published!`, then `q` over the view answers; a second `q` answers\n  identically. Use `jing-file` as W38 does; the store is genuinely closed,\n  so a lazily-faulted row would throw, which is what makes the test a pin.\n\n### Prove\n\n- `grep -c \"ds/\" src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc` → **0**; `grep -n\n  \"dao.stream\\|dao.jing\" src/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc` → **nothing**.\n- `grep -c \"ds/\" test/dao/space/schema_test.cljc` → **0**;\n  `test/dao/space/schema_fixtures.cljc` does not exist;\n  `grep -rn \"schema-fixtures\\|schema_fixtures\\|schema/published\\|dao.space.schema/published\\|dao.space.schema/current\\|PublishedSchemaRows\\|SchemaWrapper\" src test docs/design` → **nothing** (the\n  orchestrator log is history and is not edited).\n- `grep -rn \"\\[dao.stream :as\" src/cljc/dao/space` → **nothing**: every\n  `dao.space.*` namespace is off v1.\n- Three lanes and the demo compile, as Phase 1.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Host matrix\n\n| Concern | clj | cljs (Node) | cljd |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `with-write-lock` | `locking` on the state atom | `(f)` | `(f)` — unchanged, no new reader conditional |\n| Wrapper as a map (D8) | removes `^dao.space.schema.SchemaWrapper` hint | same | removes the `^dao.space.schema/SchemaWrapper` hint and the private-field trap; `@(:state w)` is host-neutral |\n| Exception types in new tests | `Exception` | `js/Error` | `Object` — the existing `#?(:cljs js/Error :cljd Object :default Exception)` idiom, `:cljd` before `:default` |\n| `concurrent-strict-unique-writes-serialize` | runs | `(is true)` | `(is true)` — unchanged |\n| `publish-serializes-against-close` | deleted | was `(is true)` | was `(is true)` |\n| V13's ring buffer | `dao.stream.ringbuffer` with `:dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity 4` | same | same — the fixture already appears in `fresh-streams` on all hosts |\n| V14's file store | `jing-file/create-content-file` | same (`fs`) | same (`dart:io`) — `materialize-to-file-store` and `cleanup-file` already carry the conditionals |\n| Test discovery | `cognitect.test-runner` over `test/` | shadow `:node-test` auto-discovers `*-test`; confirm `Testing dao.space.schema-test` appears | `clojure -M:cljd test` over `test/`; deleting `schema_fixtures` removes a non-test namespace and needs no allowlist edit |\n| Reader conditionals added by this plan | **none** | **none** | **none** — the `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` trap cannot bite code that adds no conditionals; the ones the plan deletes (`:require-macros`, the hint trio) are subtractions |\n\n---\n\n## 7. The boundary\n\n### Built here\n\n- `dao.space.schema` on the value model: `transactor` → map, `transact!`\n  returns the inner receipt, `close!`, `publish!` unguarded, `current` over\n  values with D4's completeness policy.\n- Deleted: `SchemaWrapper`, both `defopen`s, `PublishedSchemaRows`,\n  `published`, the borrowed-realization branch, `schema_fixtures`, every\n  `dao.stream`/`dao.jing`/`dao.jing.coordinate` require in `schema.cljc`.\n- `dao.space.schema.md` rewritten where §4–§5 stand; `transactor.md` T20\n  retired; `dao.stream.md`'s consumer list loses `dao.space`.\n- Five new tests (V13, V14, D1's idempotent close and order pin, D7's\n  publish-after-close); seven deleted with named reasons (V8, V10, V11, P4,\n  L9, and W15's `closed?` line, W26 renamed).\n\n### Confirmed, not assumed\n\n- **`public/demo.html`**: schema is not on its path (§0.5). The demo *is*\n  on v1 `dao.stream` through `compilation_pipeline.cljs:15-16` and\n  `artifact.cljs:5-6`; that is the \"demo and server surfaces\" line of\n  `dao.stream.md`'s consumer list and is outside this plan.\n\n### Left owing, by namespace — after this plan, `dao.space.*` is entirely v2\n\nAt the end of Phase 2, `grep -rln \"\\[dao.stream :as\" src/cljc/dao/space`\nreturns nothing, and `dao.space.query`, `dao.space.index`,\n`dao.space.transactor` require `dao.stream` while `dao.space.schema` and\n`dao.space.transact` require no stream namespace at all. **The brief's Q10\nanswer is yes.** What the rest of the repo still holds on v1 (`grep -rln\n\"\\[dao.stream :as\\|dao.stream\\]\" src`, measured at `4b9f0e7`), none of it\nplanned here:\n\n| Group | Namespaces on v1 |\n|---|---|\n| **The v1 transports** (deleted with v1, not migrated) | `dao.stream.{apply, file, file-input-stream, file-output-stream, http, link, ringbuffer, udp, ws}`, `dao.stream.rpc.{client, server, udp, ws}`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/{udp,ws}.cljd` |\n| **`yin.vm.*` lineage** (deleted under `yin.vm-consumers.implementation-plan.md`) | `yin.vm`, `yin.vm.{ast-walker, engine, ffi, register, semantic, space, stack, stream-driver, telemetry}`, `yin.repl`, `src/clj/yin/vm/{bytecode_bench, telemetry_server/jvm}`, `src/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd.cljd` |\n| **`yin.io`** with its two consumers | `yin.io.{file, file-input-stream, file-output-stream}`, `dao.gui.event`, `dao.postgraphics.terminal` |\n| **`dao.runtime`** (gated on the v1 VM's deletion) | `dao.runtime`, `dao.await` |\n| **`dao.jing`'s remote adapter and DHT node** | `dao.jing.remote` (via `dao.stream.rpc.client`/`rpc.ws`, clj only), `dao.jing.dht.node` (via `dao.stream.transit`) |\n| **Agent tooling** | `agent.tools`; `agent.tzu` is dead code and excluded from consumer analysis |\n| **Demo and server surfaces** | `datomworld.continuation-transport`, `datomworld.demo.{earth-moon-runner, voxel-runner}`, `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/{artifact, compilation_pipeline, continuation_stream, equation_plotter, solar_system, yin_repl}`, `src/cljs/datomworld/ws_client_demo`, `src/cljs/yin/vm/telemetry_viewer`, `src/clj/datomworld/ws_demo_server`, `src/cljd/datomworld/demo/{artifact, dao_gui, postgraphics, solar_system}` |\n\nPlus 45 test namespaces under `test/` requiring `[dao.stream :as ds]`,\nof which the two schema files are the only `dao.space` ones. `dao.stream`\ncannot take the name until the table above is empty; this plan empties one\nrow of `dao.stream.md`'s list and adds nothing to any other.\n\n---\n\n## 8. End condition\n\n- `dao.space.schema` requires exactly `dao.datom`, `dao.space.index`,\n  `dao.space.query`, `dao.space.transactor` — no stream namespace of either\n  generation, no `dao.jing`.\n- `schema/transact!` has one result shape, the transactor's; `schema/close!`\n  returns the v2 close outcome; there is no `closed?`, no `published`, no\n  registered opener, no `SchemaWrapper`, no `PublishedSchemaRows`, no\n  `schema_fixtures`.\n- `docs/design/dao.stream.md`'s remaining-consumer list no longer names\n  `dao.space`; `docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md` T20 is retired and its\n  schema open item deleted; `docs/design/dao.space.schema.md` §3.1, §4, §5,\n  §7, §8, §9 describe the value model and nothing else.\n- The transactor Phase 3 record's \"schema's `finally`\" note is closed by\n  D5's pointer to `query_test:1076` and `:716`.\n- Every `dao.space.*` namespace is on v2. Nothing is owed to a later plan by\n  this namespace; what remains before the rename is §7's table, each row\n  under its own plan.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
